## 1. Record-observable quotient

For a supplied completion `L`, let `Hist(L)` be the set of lawful finite record
histories. If weights are supplied, let `w_L(h)` be the history weight. Let
`I_L(h)` be every scalar readout available from the final or intermediate
record configurations.

Define

```text
L approximately_record L'
```

when the history supports agree, every supplied history weight agrees, and all
record readouts agree. In a surface with no supplied weights, the weight clause
is absent rather than silently set uniform.

### Quotient theorem

Suppose the two completions share the same supplied initial record-history
support: histories of `L` and `L'` are generated from the same set of initial
record configurations. This equal-initial-history-support premise is part of
the theorem hypothesis, not a consequence. If, in addition, a parameter or
appended operator enters none of the record-successor conditions, formation
weights, or readouts, changing it leaves the completion in the same
record-equivalence class.

**Proof.** Every finite history is generated by iterating the same successor
relation from the same initial support, hence `Hist(L)=Hist(L')`. The supplied
weight and readout functions are identical by hypothesis. These are exactly the
defining coordinates of record equivalence. QED.

Without the equal-initial-support premise the conclusion fails: two
completions with identical successor conditions, weights, and readouts but
different initial record configurations generate different history sets. The
runner exhibits this as an explicit control.

In the July-10 countermodel, the availability rule and monotone record-history
relation depend only on the recorded neighbor projectors, while
`lambda sum_edges(I-SWAP)` is appended independently and readout is record
count. (The appended exchange family acts on the qubit carrier; its
restriction to the one-excitation sector is `lambda` times the cubic graph
Laplacian. The Laplacian identification is sector-restricted, not an identity
on the full carrier.) Therefore every `lambda`, including zero, is
record-equivalent on that completion.

This is an operational theorem, not an ontological deletion rule. Concluding
that record-null structure is not physical requires the separately proposed
record-influence criterion or another ontology principle.

