## 2. Same-carrier permanence obstruction

Let `K` be finite-dimensional and let
`H in B(C^2_x tensor K)` be bounded. Let `{P}` range over a supplied family of
recordable rank-one projectors at site `x`, and suppose permanence is
implemented by

```text
[H, P tensor I_K] = 0                                      (1)
```

for every recordable `P`. The theorem additionally uses the supplied condition

> **Recordable-frame spanning (supplied, zero premise weight).** The
> recordable rank-one frames at the site linearly span `M_2(C)`.

The four axioms do not determine which rank-one possibilities are recordable
at a site, so this spanning statement enters as a supplied condition and gains
no framework support here. Under it, linearity extends (1) to
`[H, M tensor I_K]=0` for every `M in M_2(C)`, and the standard
finite-dimensional matrix-factor commutant theorem gives

```text
H in I_x tensor B(K).                                      (2)
```

Thus `H` cannot act nontrivially on that same site carrier.

On two qubits, the runner imposes (1) for four recordable frames that are
verified to linearly span `M_2(C)`. The exact linear system has rank `12` on
`16` operator coordinates and a four-dimensional nullspace, exactly
`I tensor M_2(C)`; each nullspace element is verified directly to have the
`I tensor B` form, and a non-spanning two-frame control leaves a strictly
larger commutant, so the spanning condition is load-bearing. `SWAP` fails (1),
but so does ordinary number-conserving hopping. Therefore this permanence
demand is not a Dirac selector: it freezes the scalar and first-order
transport branches alike.

## 3. Finite-unitary formation obstruction

Let a finite carrier split as

```text
H_total = H_blank direct-sum H_record,
```

with equal finite input/output sector dimensions, and let `U` be unitary. If
permanence means

```text
U H_record subset H_record,                                (3)
```

then the restriction of `U` to `H_record` is an isometry from a finite space
to itself and hence onto. Therefore `H_record` is reducing, its orthogonal
complement is invariant, and

```text
P_record U P_blank = 0.                                    (4)
```

No blank state forms a record. A unitary sector swap supplies a nonzero
blank-to-record block, but maps old records back to blank and violates (3).

The quantitative content behind (4) is the exact identity, valid for every
unitary `U` and every orthogonal split,

```text
||P_record U P_blank||_F = ||P_blank U P_record||_F,       (4')
```

so the formation-block norm always equals the record-leakage norm, and
invariance (zero leakage) forces zero formation. The runner verifies (4')
symbolically on the general one-blank/one-record cell, numerically on sampled
higher-dimensional unitaries, and shows the identity fails for a non-unitary
control, so the check reads the unitarity hypothesis rather than the displayed
witnesses.

This obstruction is finite-dimensional and same-carrier scoped. It does not
exclude an enlarged environment, an infinite shift, a time-dependent fresh
ancilla stream, conditional gating, or a nonunitary record-level channel.

## 4. Rank-one locked-output outcome-operation normal form

Let `J_P` be a finite-dimensional completely positive trace-nonincreasing
outcome operation such that for every positive input `rho`, its output is
supported in a fixed rank-one record projector `P=|p><p|`:

```text
(I-P) J_P(rho) (I-P) = 0.                                  (5)
```

By the standard finite-dimensional Kraus representation theorem, write
`J_P(rho)=sum_j K_j rho K_j^dagger`. For every
pure input, positivity prevents cancellation of support outside `P`; hence
each `K_j` has range in `P` and can be written

```text
K_j = |p><v_j|.
```

Consequently

```text
J_P(rho) = Tr(E_P rho) P,
E_P = sum_j |v_j><v_j|,                                    (6)
```

with `0 <= E_P <= I`. Conversely every such effect defines a CP
trace-nonincreasing locked-output operation by (6). The effect is unique
because `Tr(E_P rho)=Tr(J_P(rho))` for every `rho`.

The runner checks both directions of this normal form on the qubit cell: the
forward direction by imposing the locked-output condition (5) on a fully
generic symbolic Kraus operator and deriving `K=|p><v|` as the unique
solution (with an explicit control operator violating (5)), and the converse
by verifying (6) for a symbolic two-operator family.

Thus the supplied locked-output condition fixes the outcome-operation form,
while the formation weight is

```text
w(P|rho) = Tr(E_P rho).                                    (7)
```

This does not construct a full instrument and does not derive `E_P=P`. A full
instrument requires a family of outcome operations plus joint normalization,
for example `sum_P E_P=I` for an exhaustive discrete instrument. The runner
constructs inequivalent positive effects satisfying the same permanence and
locked-output requirements.
Obtaining the Lueders/Born special case requires an additional effect-selection
theorem, such as a successfully derived composite-menu frame-function bridge.

